s one correct choice.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [], \"failing_templates\": [], \"overall\": \"PASS\", \"reasoning\": \"Every family draws from fixed text or from _ITEM_POOL, which is one row per problem. F01 to F05 are hints using only cumulative vocabulary and the gated word 'side' printed as 'sides'. The hint statements about circles, rectangles, squares and triangles are accurate. F02 mentions opposite sides being equal; 'opposite' is in neither NOT_YET_KNOWN nor cumulative_vocab, and the rectangle property is consistent with the shape vocabulary allowed here, so I do not fail it. F06 and F09 print the keyed answer, which is circle, half-circle or quarter-circle, and each bank row keys a member of that set. F07 prints option keys A to D. F08 and F10 print distractors that are each wrong for their row's question (for example, whole circle is wrong when a row keys a part of a circle). Each row's question (F11) is accurate: the circle row, the half-circle rows and the quarter-circle rows match their keyed answers. The printed forms are hyphenated (half-circle, quarter-circle), while the curriculum vocabulary is unhyphenated (half circle, quarter circle); this is a spelling variant of allowed words and is not wrong or unreadable, so it is not a failure. No family prints a word outside cumulative_vocab, and no family exceeds the grade 2 Q1 scope of circles, half circles and quarter circles.\"}",
